  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 247 AM EDT Sun May 24 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis: \n\n500mb shortwave troughing is currently over Southern Ontario with \nits axis lifting across the Great Lakes Region this morning. Besides \nthe mostly cloudy skies continuing today, enough vorticity aloft and \nlift associated with the departing surface low pressure across the \nnorthwoods will support a weak line of showers sweeping across the \nCWA before dry air moves into the backside of this system. \n\nAt the same time, upstream midlevel ridging begins to build to the \nlee of the American Rockies and deepen over central North America. \nThis pattern will attempt to build an omega-style blocking pattern \nand remain in place over the next several days. This supports \nsubsidence aloft and surface high pressure across the Great Lakes \nRegion. Little to no precipitation is expected at this time as \ntemperatures gradually warm above normal for late May/early June.  \n\nForecast Details:\n\nToday...Surface low pressure will be positioned over Michigan\u2019s \nThumb this morning while lifting to the northeast. A disheveled line \nof showers will sweep across the lower peninsula with enough \nmoisture to deliver a few hundredths of measurable rain mainly to \nareas east of I-75. Model soundings over the CWA show conditions \ndrying from the top parts of the atmosphere downward via subsidence \nthis afternoon/evening, eventually killing all precip. Cool air \ndrawn in from the north on the backside of this system will keep \nhigh temperatures in the upper 50s to low 60s today before gradually \nwarming this week.\n\nTuesday through Saturday...An omega-style blocking pattern develops \nover North America by the midweek. The Great Lakes region will \nremain positioned on the right hand side of the ridge with midlevel \ntroughing downstream over the northeastern U.S. Surface high \npressure centered over the Northern Plains will support dry and \nquiet weather for the next several days with temperatures building \ninto the midweek. Highs in the upper 50s/low 60s today will climb \ninto the low/mid 80s for most areas by Tuesday and remain above \naverage through the remainder of the forecast period. The only \nconcern in terms of impactful weather is the typical favorable fire \nweather concerns from afternoon mixing leading to high dew point \ndepressions. At this time, the driest days remain beyond the midweek \nas the ridge shifts eastward directly over the upper Midwest.",
